   alongside the mearcher in their familiar surroundings, discusing their
   emotions, localised victimisation experiencem, and community concerns.
   Additionally. groups allow for a deeper analysis specific situations,
   community ties, and opinions complaints regarding local government
   performance. This information is triangulated using non participant and
   participant observations as well as in depth interviews. Finally,
   perceptions are synthesised into Intensity of Fear Maps Talk's Track
   Maps while also being available form of databases, Babies, and
   narratives.<br /> Combining geographical information systems with
   qualitative analysis, such as thematic ysis, can capture spatial
   people's perceptions without losing rich narratives. This method
   generates the Talk's track map a tool that enables the georeferencing of
   quali tative coding flom mobile interviews.<br /> Variety of
   datacollection and analysis methods allows to efficiently triangulate
   research about built engironment and perceptions.},
